# Summary
Introduced: the commit added tools/spout-capture/CMakeLists.txt with FetchContent pulling Spout2 by mutable tag and added release workflow steps that build and package the resulting executable. This creates a new supply-chain/release-integrity risk.
The new Spout helper build uses CMake FetchContent to clone Spout2 from https://github.com/leadedge/Spout2.git with GIT_TAG set to the tag-like string 2.007.015 instead of a full immutable commit SHA or vendored source with hash verification. FetchContent processes the fetched project's CMake code during the release job. Because the release workflow runs this build before packaging, signing, and publishing, a compromised upstream repository/tag or maliciously moved tag could execute code on the release runner, modify the helper or other release artifacts, and ship a malicious spout-capture.exe inside the official zip. The helper is explicitly copied into the release package. The later signing step signs only ClipForVRChat.exe, not the helper executable or the whole zip, so users verifying the detached exe signature would not detect a tampered helper.

# Validation
## Rubric
- [x] Confirm the commit introduced a new helper CMake build using FetchContent for an external dependency.
- [x] Confirm the dependency is pinned by a mutable tag-like value rather than an immutable commit SHA or verified archive hash.
- [x] Confirm the release workflow executes the helper CMake configure/build before packaging.
- [x] Confirm the helper executable is copied into and required in the official release package.
- [x] Confirm release signing covers only ClipForVRChat.exe and not spout-capture.exe or the full zip.
## Report
Validated the finding as a release-integrity/supply-chain issue rather than a memory-safety crash. Evidence: `tools/spout-capture/CMakeLists.txt:4-14` includes CMake FetchContent, fetches `https://github.com/leadedge/Spout2.git`, and pins only `GIT_TAG 2.007.015`, not an immutable commit SHA or hash-verified archive. Running the actual project configure command showed CMake immediately attempts to populate `spout2` by cloning that external repository during configure: output included `-- Populating spout2`, `Performing download step (git clone)`, and `Failed to clone repository: 'https://github.com/leadedge/Spout2.git'` due the container network policy. The release workflow executes this path in `.github/workflows/release.yml:65-69` using `cmake -S tools/spout-capture ...` and then builds target `spout-capture`. The resulting helper is copied into the official package at `.github/workflows/release.yml:142-146`, required in validation at lines `235-242`, zipped at line `183`, and uploaded at lines `335-339`. The signing step at `.github/workflows/release.yml:155-176` detaches a GPG signature only for `dist/ClipForVRChat/ClipForVRChat.exe`; no detached signature is produced for `spout-capture.exe` or for the zip as a whole. To demonstrate exploitability of the exact FetchContent/tag pattern, I created a local PoC where a victim CMake project uses `FetchContent_Declare(... GIT_TAG 2.007.015)` against a local stand-in Spout2 repo. First configure fetched a benign tag and executed benign CMake code. Then I force-moved the same tag name to a new commit with CMake code that writes `MOVED_TAG_CODE_EXECUTED.txt`; a clean second configure fetched `HEAD is now at ... moved-tag-malicious` and produced marker `MALICIOUS CMAKE CODE EXECUTED FROM MOVED TAG`. This confirms that a moved/compromised upstream tag can execute changed CMake code in the release build without any repository change here. Crash/valgrind/debugger validation is not applicable because this finding is not a runtime memory-corruption bug; the relevant execution primitive is build-time CMake code execution.

