# Attack-path analysis
Final: high | Decider: model_decided | Matrix severity: high | Policy adjusted: high
## Rationale
Kept as high. The finding is real and in scope: tools/spout-capture/CMakeLists.txt fetches a mutable external Spout2 tag, the release workflow builds it, packages the resulting helper, and uploads it as an official release asset. The impact is major because malicious upstream CMake/build content can alter shipped executable artifacts and the helper is later executed by the desktop app's Spout capture path. However, the likelihood is not high enough for critical because exploitation requires compromise/control of an external upstream dependency or mutable tag and a subsequent maintainer release build; it is not a directly exposed public service or 0-click path against installed users. Existing controls such as read-only build contents permission and signing of the main executable reduce but do not eliminate the risk, especially because the helper and full zip are not signed.
## Likelihood
high - The vulnerable path is automatically reached during official release builds, but an attacker needs a non-trivial precondition: control of the upstream Spout2 repository/tag or an equivalent dependency-delivery compromise before maintainers publish a release. This is plausible supply-chain risk, not a directly internet-exposed unauthenticated application endpoint. | Remote network vector
## Impact
high - A successful attack can execute code in the release build and ship a malicious bundled executable through the official release zip. Because the helper is executed by product functionality and is not separately covered by the detached signature, this can lead to end-user code execution under the user's account and potentially release-signing workflow abuse if future-step environment/path poisoning is achieved.
## Assumptions
- The GitHub Actions release workflow is the authoritative path for producing official Windows release assets.
- CMake FetchContent processes the fetched Spout2 project's CMake/build logic during configure/build.
- An attacker must gain control over the upstream Spout2 repository, the referenced tag, or an equivalent dependency-delivery path before a release build runs.
- GitHub Actions standard environment behavior allows build steps to affect the workspace and potentially future steps via files and workflow environment/path mechanisms, although this was not dynamically tested against GitHub Actions.
- Compromise or control of https://github.com/leadedge/Spout2.git or mutable tag 2.007.015
- A maintainer-triggered release build after the upstream tag/repository is malicious
- Release consumers download or run the official zip containing the helper
